On your Bastion node, create a new folder structure called my-nginx-chart:
mkdir -p my-nginx-chart/templates

2. Create Chart.yaml
This file satisfies the metadata requirement that Helm was looking for earlier. Create it at the root of your chart directory:
my-nginx-chart/Chart.yaml
apiVersion: v2 
name: my-nginx-chart 
description: A reusable Nginx web deployment 
type: application 
version: 0.1.0 
appVersion: "1.25"

3. Create values.yaml
This file isolates the variables (like replica counts and image tags) so you can change them easily without modifying the core code.
my-nginx-chart/values.yaml
replicaCount: 1 
image: 
repository: nginx 
tag: latest 
pullPolicy: Always 
service: 
type: NodePort 
port: 80 
targetPort: 80

4. Create the Parameterized Templates
Now, we will split your original manifest into two clean templates inside the templates/ directory, replacing the hardcoded text fields with Helm variables ({{ ... }}).
my-nginx-chart/templates/deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-nginx
  namespace: {{ .Release.Namespace }}
spec:
  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: {{ .Release.Name }}-nginx
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: {{ .Release.Name }}-nginx
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: nginx
        image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag }}"
        imagePullPolicy: {{ .Values.image.pullPolicy }}
        ports:
        - containerPort: {{ .Values.service.targetPort }}

The Service Template:
my-nginx-chart/templates/service.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-service
  namespace: {{ .Release.Namespace }}
spec:
  type: {{ .Values.service.type }}
  selector:
    app: {{ .Release.Name }}-nginx
  ports:
  - name: http
    port: {{ .Values.service.port }}
    targetPort: {{ .Values.service.targetPort }}


5. Deploy with Helm!
Now that your project matches a legitimate Helm blueprint layout, you can verify it and install it smoothly from your Bastion node.
First, use tree to make sure your workspace matches this look:
tree my-nginx-chart
 
(It should neatly show Chart.yaml, values.yaml, and the templates/ subdirectory).
To run the installation, provide Helm with a custom Release Name (my-web-release) followed by the chart folder path:
helm install my-web-release ./my-nginx-chart

To see your running Helm release details later, simply run:
helm list

In Helm, {{ .Release.Name }} is a built-in variable that represents the unique name you give to a specific deployment instance when you run the helm install command.
It acts as a dynamic prefix or identifier so you can deploy the exact same Helm Chart multiple times to the same cluster without their names colliding
How it Works in Practice
Think back to the installation command you use:
helm install my-web-release ./my-nginx-chart
In this command, my-web-release becomes the .Release.Name.

 

Why is this useful?
It allows for instant environment replication. If you want to deploy a second copy of the exact same Nginx chart for a different developer or purpose, you don't have to change a single line of code inside the chart. You just change the release name at execution:
helm install staging-app ./my-nginx-chart
This time, Helm automatically renders the resource name as staging-app-nginx. It keeps your Kubernetes resources perfectly organized and prevents "resource already exists" errors!

---
.Release.Name, {{ .Release.Namespace }} is another built-in Helm variable. It automatically represents the Kubernetes namespace where the chart is being deployed.
Instead of hardcoding namespace: default or namespace: k8s-dashboard into your YAML files, you use this variable so the chart adapts dynamically based on your command line input.
 
Helm dynamically injects namespace: dev-environment.

If you don't specify a namespace flag in your command:
helm install my-web-release ./my-nginx-chart
Helm will automatically fall back to whatever namespace your current kubectl context is actively set to (usually default).

